#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gs_wake_keys - the VAULT's half of pairing. Run it on the ThinkPad, once.

    ThinkPad:  python3 gs_wake_keys pair
    Pi:        python3 gs_doorbell pair <the address the ThinkPad printed>

Two commands, one code to compare, one y on each screen. That is the whole
ceremony, and it is short on purpose: every step an operator can get wrong is a
step this design has to defend, and the cheapest defence is not to have it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The first version minted BOTH keypairs here and told the operator to carry the
Pi's SECRET across on a USB stick, then shred it. Three copies of one secret
existed at once -- this disk, the stick, the Pi -- and destroying two of them
was homework. The instruction was correct. Nobody follows instructions at 2am,
and a stick with a wake secret on it is indistinguishable from any other stick
in the drawer six months later.

Now each box generates its own keypair, in place, and only PUBLIC keys cross
the LAN. There is no moment at which a secret exists anywhere but on the
machine that will use it, so there is nothing for the operator to clean up.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
KERCKHOFFS, AND WHY THIS BOX'S KEYFILE IS NOT ENCRYPTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
This repository is public. The adversary has read it: they know the file names,
the ports, the record size, the job vocabulary and this docstring. Only key
material may be secret.

The Pi's keyfile IS sealed with a passphrase (Argon2id + XSalsa20-Poly1305),
because an SD card leaves the building in a pocket and the Pi is poked by a
person who can type. This box's keyfile is NOT, and the reason is not
laziness:

    gs_wake_agent runs at boot, unattended, on a machine whose disk unlocks
    itself so that it CAN boot unattended. Any key that process can read, a
    thief holding the powered-on laptop can read. A passphrase the machine
    stores in order to type it to itself is not a passphrase.

So this file says `"kdf": "none"` and stores its payload as plain JSON, rather
than dressing plaintext up as a ciphertext. What that costs is bounded and is
stated here rather than discovered: someone who takes this laptop can

  * impersonate this vault to the doorbell -- and collect a queued job, which
    tells them a job was queued and nothing else;
  * decrypt recorded M1 and M3 records -- an ephemeral public key, a random
    challenge, a job id and a four-character label. No address, no amount, no
    memo: those are in M2, which is sealed to a per-boot ephemeral key that
    only ever existed in the RAM of a machine that has since powered off.

They also have the wallet, which is a far larger problem and is what
OPSEC_SETUP.md section 6 already scores. Rotating this pairing afterwards is
one command on each box.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS TOOL REFUSES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * to overwrite an existing keyfile, because silently replacing a keypair
    breaks the other box in a way that presents as "the doorbell stopped
    working" -- byte-identical to a dead switch, a reset BIOS and a hostile WOL
  * to write anywhere group- or world-readable
  * to guess this machine's MAC. It detects it from the interface that carries
    the route to the Pi, and refuses with instructions if it cannot
  * to run at all without PyNaCl
"""
